---@class posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSchemaOutput
---@field id? string
---@field name? string
---@field label? string|distilled.Null
---@field table? posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSchemaOutputTableMap|distilled.Null
---@field table_id string|distilled.Null The id of the warehouse table this schema syncs into. Set before the first sync for a table the schema already names, such as a migrated one. Null when there is none.
---@field should_sync? boolean
---@field last_synced_at? string|distilled.Null
---@field latest_error? string|distilled.Null The latest error that occurred when syncing this schema.
---@field incremental? boolean
---@field status? string|distilled.Null
---@field pending_self_managed_table boolean Whether this schema is linked to an existing self-managed table that its first sync will import. Until then the table keeps reading the original bucket.
---@field sync_type? posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSchemaSyncTypeEnum|distilled.Null Sync strategy: incremental, full_refresh, append, cdc, or xmin. * `full_refresh` - full_refresh * `incremental` - incremental * `append` - append * `webhook` - webhook * `cdc` - cdc * `xmin` - xmin
---@field incremental_field? string|distilled.Null Column name used to track sync progress.
---@field incremental_field_type? posthog.external_data_sources.IncrementalFieldTypeEnum|distilled.Null Data type of the incremental field. * `integer` - integer * `numeric` - numeric * `datetime` - datetime * `date` - date * `timestamp` - timestamp * `objectid` - objectid * `xid` - xid
---@field incremental_field_lookback_seconds? number|distilled.Null Seconds to subtract from the stored incremental watermark at sync time, so each incremental run re-reads a rolling overlap window and catches late or backdated rows. Applies to timestamp/date incremental fields only. The stored watermark is unchanged. Maximum 5184000 (60 days).
---@field sync_frequency? posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSchemaSyncFrequencyEnum|distilled.Null How often to sync. The fastest sync frequency is 5 minutes. * `never` - never * `5min` - 5min * `15min` - 15min * `30min` - 30min * `1hour` - 1hour * `6hour` - 6hour * `12hour` - 12hour * `24hour` - 24hour * `7day` - 7day * `30day` - 30day
---@field sync_time_of_day? string|distilled.Null UTC time of day to run the sync (HH:MM:SS).
---@field full_refresh_interval_days? number|distilled.Null Days between scheduled full refreshes, from 1 to 90, or null for none. A full refresh wipes the table and re-imports every row, so rows deleted at the source are removed. It runs on the first scheduled sync once the interval has passed, counted from when it was saved or from the last full resync (or, when full_refresh_time_of_day is set, from the slot of that time the last refresh served), and can start up to an hour early. Queries keep returning the current rows until a full refresh finishes, and workflows and destinations that run on new rows of the table run again for every row. Available for incremental, append, and xmin syncs only, and never shorter than the sync frequency.
---@field full_refresh_time_of_day? string|distilled.Null UTC time of day (HH:MM:SS) that scheduled full refreshes are due, for example outside working hours. The refresh runs on the first scheduled sync from up to an hour before this time, so on a table that syncs every few hours it can run hours later. Each interval counts from the slot of this time that the last refresh or save served, where a slot less than an hour away counts as served. Saving a new time restarts the clock, so the first refresh after a save can come up to a day before a full interval has passed. Null counts the interval from when it was saved or from the last full resync. Cleared when full_refresh_interval_days is null.
---@field next_full_refresh_at? string|distilled.Null When the next scheduled full refresh is due. The first scheduled sync that starts at most an hour before this time re-imports the table. Saving a new interval or time, or any full resync, moves it one interval ahead, onto full_refresh_time_of_day when that is set.
---@field description? string|distilled.Null
---@field primary_key_columns? posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSchemaOutputPrimaryKeyColumnsList|distilled.Null Column names for primary key deduplication.
---@field cdc_table_mode? posthog.external_data_sources.CdcTableModeEnum|distilled.Null For CDC syncs: consolidated, cdc_only, or both. * `consolidated` - consolidated * `cdc_only` - cdc_only * `both` - both
---@field incremental_sync_blocked? posthog.external_data_sources.IncrementalSyncBlockedReasonEnum|distilled.Null Why the last sync run could not merge rows for this table, or `null` when no such failure is current, which includes a run that failed for another reason. A blocked table is disabled, and the resolution differs by reason. `missing_primary_key`: no key to merge on, so set `primary_key_columns` to a unique key, which is accepted because none was set before. `duplicate_primary_key`: the key in use does not identify one row, and that key cannot be swapped once data has synced, so either remove the duplicates at the source and set `should_sync` to true, or delete the synced data before setting a different key. Either reason also accepts a different `sync_type`: `append` is only safe for insert-only tables, because updated rows arrive again as duplicates, and `full_refresh` re-reads the whole table on every sync and bills every row. This reports the last run's failure, so it clears once a run succeeds or fails for another reason, not when an update lands. * `missing_primary_key` - Missing primary key * `duplicate_primary_key` - Duplicate primary key
---@field webhook_not_registered boolean Whether this table uses webhook sync, but its source has no enabled webhook. Such a table gets no webhook updates. Each sync polls the source API instead, which is slower and can read the whole table. Set up the webhook on the source, or change `sync_type`.
---@field enabled_columns? posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSchemaOutputEnabledColumnsList|distilled.Null Names of source columns to sync. `null` (default) syncs all columns. Primary-key columns and the active incremental field are always retained, even if not listed here.
---@field masked_columns? posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSchemaOutputMaskedColumnsList|distilled.Null Names of source columns whose values are replaced with a deterministic one-way digest at sync time, for sensitive data such as passwords or PII. `null` (default) masks nothing. Primary-key, partition and incremental columns can't be masked, and direct query sources don't support masking. Any change to this list starts a full resync of the table (CDC tables take a new snapshot), or stages it until syncing is turned on. A synced webhook table can't change its masked columns, because its data can't be fetched again.
---@field row_filters? posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSchemaOutputRowFiltersList|distilled.Null Predicates ANDed onto the source query so only matching rows sync. Each is `{column, operator, value}`; `null`/empty (default) syncs all rows. The operator must be one of `> >= < <= = != IN "NOT IN"` and the value must match the column's type (for `IN`/`NOT IN`, a comma-separated list like `1, 2, 3` or `'a','b'`). Applied on the next sync — not retroactive to already-synced rows.
---@field row_filter_columns posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSchemaOutputRowFilterColumnsList|distilled.Null Columns a row filter on this schema may use, with the operators each accepts. `null` means any column in `available_columns` with any operator, which is the case for SQL sources. A list means the source can filter on these columns only; an empty list means this schema accepts no row filter.
---@field available_columns? posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSchemaOutputAvailableColumnsList Column metadata (name, data type, nullable) for this schema. For SQL sources this is the source-side schema discovered via `refresh_schemas`; for other sources (and once synced) it falls back to the synced table's columns. Empty only before the first successful sync/refresh.
---@field source_column_metadata_available? boolean Whether exact source-side column metadata is available for safe source-query projection.
---@field history_table_name string|distilled.Null Name of the history table that keeps every version of each row, or `null` when the table keeps no history. A history table created before `_history` names existed keeps its `_cdc` name.
---@field registered_history_table_name string|distilled.Null Name of the history table registered for this schema, whether or not its mode still writes it, or `null` when none is registered. One the schema no longer writes can be removed with the `history_table` delete endpoint. Populated on the single-schema retrieve and update endpoints, `null` elsewhere. After an update that sent `drop_history_table`, a name means the history table was kept, for example because a sync started meanwhile.
---@field source? posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSchemaOutputSource|distilled.Null Lightweight parent-source summary (id, source_type, access_method, column-selection support, the requesting user's access level). Only populated on the single-schema retrieve endpoint — `null` elsewhere — so read-only views can render without fetching the full source and all its schemas.
---@field api_version? string|distilled.Null Vendor API version override for this schema. `null` (default) syncs on the source's pinned version. Must be one of the source type's supported versions. User-managed: version-migration tooling never changes it. Not available for webhook-sync schemas.
---@field api_version_deprecation? posthog.external_data_sources.ExternalDataSourceApiVersionDeprecation|distilled.Null Set when this schema's version override is deprecated by the vendor; null when there is no override or it is not deprecated. The source-level field covers the source pin.
---@field user_access_level? string|distilled.Null The effective access level the user has for this object

